{0 httpkit-core}

Checked HTTP values with no runtime dependencies beyond OCaml's standard library.
The library performs no I/O and does not own body lifetimes.

{1 Constructing a request}

Validation returns bounded {!Httpkit_core.Error.t} diagnostics. Handle them at
the boundary where untrusted strings enter your application:

{[
open Httpkit_core
let ( let* ) = Result.bind
let request path =
  let* target = Target.of_string path in
  let* headers = Headers.of_list ["accept", "application/json"] in
  Ok (Request.create ~meth:Method.get ~target ~headers ())
]}

{!Httpkit_core.Request.map_body} and {!Httpkit_core.Response.map_body} can change
the body type without interpreting it. A body may be a string, a stream, or an
application value. Runtime adapters will own stream operations.

{1 Contracts}

- {!Httpkit_core.Method} preserves case and accepts extension tokens.
- {!Httpkit_core.Header} normalizes names to lowercase; values preserve opaque
  bytes and reject injection characters. Surrounding whitespace is rejected.
- {!Httpkit_core.Headers} preserves duplicate fields and their order, with
  explicit field-count and byte budgets. Values are never implicitly joined.
- {!Httpkit_core.Target} preserves raw percent escapes. It validates lexical
  tokens, not URI semantics, target forms, authority, or filesystem safety.
- {!Httpkit_core.Status} accepts codes from 100 through 599.

All lengths and diagnostic offsets count bytes, not Unicode characters. Defaults
are project resource policies and may be explicitly overridden at construction.
Collections impose an additional aggregate budget even on prevalidated fields.

{1 Limits of this package}

There is no wire parser or serializer yet. A request assembled from these values
still needs checks for method/target compatibility, Host, conflicting framing,
and body semantics. These belong to the upcoming HTTP/1 codec and engine. Core
does not reject duplicate Content-Length fields or infer Transfer-Encoding.

{!modules:Httpkit_core}
